{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Or who makes mute.</b> Muteness is the absence of speech, and the term שימה does not apply to it. But since in man there is a speaking soul, and the condition of muteness in the mute is that he be blocked in the tendons of the tongue — and because of this weakening — therefore the term שימה is used of it." "<b>And in the Guide,</b> it is written that for one who removes a possession, the term for the act of removal is used — just as they say of one who extinguishes a lamp that he 'brought darkness'; accordingly. And as for what He said, 'or deaf' — He should have said 'or hearing or deaf,' so that it would be a thing and its opposite like all the rest. But because the sense of hearing is bound up with speech — for we would not know the word he speaks until we hear him speak, and if so, hearing is included in 'who sets a mouth for a man' — therefore there was no need to mention 'or hearing,' and He mentioned 'or deaf,' which is its opposite." "<b>And you should reflect</b> on how Scripture arranged three faculties, one above the other: first, speech, which is from the side of the soul, whose opposite is muteness; second, the sense of hearing, which is superior to the sense of sight — this is what He said, 'or deaf'; and third, the sense of sight, which is what He said, 'or open or blind.' And this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 15:30): ״מאור עינים ישמח לב שמועה טובה תדשן עצם״ — he explained that light broadens the heart, but the sense of hearing is far greater, for besides that it broadens the heart and gladdens it, it also fattens the bones. See how great is the benefit that reaches man through the ear more than through the eye; and so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'one blinded in his eye receives the value of his eye; one deafened receives the value of his whole being.' And because Scripture explained that the ear is a weightier organ than the eye, it is written after it (Proverbs 15:31): ״אוזן שומעת תוכחת חיים״." "<b>And know that</b> according to this, a difficulty will occur to you: why did Leah our mother precede Reuben to Simeon — for since she named them, one after sight and one after hearing, she should have given the firstborn the name of hearing. And this matter cannot be clarified except by way of the Kabbalah, for sight is from the power of the letter ה and hearing is from the power of the letter ו, and therefore she needed to give the firstborn the name of sight, in order to precede the letter ה to the letter ו in the order of א״ב ג״ד. And understand this." "<b>Is it not I, YHWH.</b> It is possible to say that He mentioned this Name in reference to understanding (בינה), of which it is written (Job 32): ״ונשמת שדי תבינם״ — corresponding to it is the speaking faculty in the soul of man through the organ of the tongue. And your sign is (Psalms 45:2–3): ״אומר אני מעשי למלך לשוני עט סופר מהיר״. And the discerning one will understand, and it will be explained to him the elevation of man in his wondrous formation. And I have already mentioned something of this at the verse (Genesis 1): ״נעשה אדם״, by way of the fourth."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:12.949788830Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}